---
// Documents the per-dataset private-holdout split (20–40% private) enforced
// by `synthbench.private_holdout` and surfaced in the leaderboard as the
// "verified" / "flagged" verification badge.
---

<section class="w-full max-w-4xl mx-auto py-12" id="private-holdout">
  <h2
    class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-text dark:text-text-dark mb-2"
  >
    Private holdout split
  </h2>
  <p class="text-muted mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    Every holdout-enabled dataset is deterministically split into a public
    subset and a private subset. The private fraction depends on dataset size,
    chosen to preserve statistical power: 20% for large datasets (OpinionsQA,
    WVS, GSS), 30% for medium ones (SubPOP, GlobalOpinionQA), and 40% for
    small ones (Michigan, NTIA, Eurobarometer). Per-question human
    distributions for the private subset are removed from every public
    SynthBench artifact (the site, the run-detail JSON, and the
    question-explorer pages), so a submitter can't recover the private
    answer key from our published files.
  </p>
  <p class="text-muted mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    Submissions must include results for every question, public and
    private. We (the SynthBench maintainers) score the private subset
    locally against the withheld answer key and publish the resulting
    <span class="font-mono">sps_private</span> alongside
    <span class="font-mono">sps_public</span>. Rows whose public/private
    SPS delta is within a tolerance of 0.05 get a
    <span
      class="mx-1 inline-block text-xs px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400"
      >✓ verified</span
    > badge. Rows outside the tolerance are
    <span
      class="mx-1 inline-block text-xs px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-amber-500/10 text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400"
      >⚠ flagged</span
    > for review.
  </p>
  <p class="text-muted mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    The partition is derived from an unsalted SHA-256 hash of <span
      class="font-mono">dataset_name + ":" + question_key</span
    >. That makes it stable across runs, machines, and Python versions
    without needing a seed file. It also means the list of private questions
    is not secret. The hash is public code, run artifacts carry explicit
    <span class="font-mono">is_holdout</span> flags, and anyone can compute
    any question's membership. This is deliberate and follows the Kaggle
    model: what is withheld is the private subset's answer key (the
    per-question human distributions), not the list of held-out questions.
  </p>
  <p class="text-muted mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    <strong>Limits.</strong> The source surveys behind these datasets are
    publicly downloadable upstream, and our open-source adapters will
    rebuild the full per-question distributions, private subset included,
    for anyone who runs them. A determined actor can therefore reconstruct
    the private targets. The holdout is a fabrication detector and a
    deterrent against casual overfitting. It does not provide cryptographic
    secrecy. It raises the effort from "copy our published JSON" to
    "reprocess the upstream source". A holdout that models cannot memorize
    would need newly fielded human data on a rotating schedule, which is a
    separate data-acquisition program.
  </p>
  <p class="text-muted max-w-3xl">
    <strong>Why have a holdout.</strong> There are two separate reasons.
    First, future LLMs may train on SynthBench itself, and holding out
    20–40% per dataset slows that loop. Second, and more important since
    per-question data was made public: once per-question human
    distributions are visible, a bad actor could fabricate a submission
    JSON that matches them exactly. The private subset is a cheap way to
    catch this. A fabricator has no signal on the hidden keys, so their
    public and private SPS diverge sharply.
  </p>

  <div id="verification-badges" class="mt-8 max-w-3xl scroll-mt-24">
    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-text dark:text-text-dark mb-2">
      Reading the verified / flagged badges
    </h3>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      Every leaderboard row on a holdout-enabled dataset carries one of two
      badges, computed at publish time by comparing the row's SPS on the
      public subset against its SPS on the private subset
      (<span class="font-mono">|sps_public − sps_private|</span>, the
      published <span class="font-mono">sps_public_private_delta</span>):
    </p>
    <ul class="text-muted mb-4 space-y-3 list-none pl-0">
      <li>
        <span
          class="mr-1 inline-block text-xs px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400"
          >✓ verified</span
        >: the public and private scores are within the 0.05 SPS divergence
        threshold. This is what an honest run looks like. The deltas we have
        observed on honest submissions are typically below 0.02.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span
          class="mr-1 inline-block text-xs px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-amber-500/10 text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400"
          >⚠ flagged</span
        >: the public/private divergence exceeded the 0.05 threshold, so
        the row is held for review.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      <strong>What a flag means.</strong> A flag comes from a statistical
      anomaly detector and is not proof of cheating. A large divergence is
      what fabrication looks like (a fabricator can match the public answer
      key but has no signal on the hidden one). It can also come from
      overfitting to the public subset or, on datasets with a small private
      subset, from ordinary sampling noise concentrated in a few questions.
      Treat a flagged score with caution, and rely on the row's
      <span class="font-mono">sps_private</span> when deciding how much to
      trust it. A flag is not an accusation against the submitter.
    </p>
    <p class="text-muted">
      The threshold is deliberately loose (0.05, versus typical honest
      deltas under 0.02) so that normal run-to-run noise does not flag
      honest submissions. Both scores and the delta are published on each
      row, so you can apply a stricter or looser standard yourself.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
